    {
      "id": 131140,
      "name": "The Orange Mailman",
      "username": "The_Orange_Mailman",
      "avatar_template": "/letter_avatar_proxy/v4/letter/t/c68b51/{size}.png",
      "created_at": "2015-02-17T00:33:51.000Z",
      "cooked": "<p>The more I look at this, the more it doesn't make sense.&nbsp; Spiff's clarifications state that Play, Draw, and Discard always trigger a shuffle.&nbsp; Just because it says \"Play the top card\", that doesn't mean it's not playing a card.&nbsp; If there is no top card, shouldn't there be a shuffle in order to play a card?</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>This language has been around since the beginning.&nbsp; Citizen Dawn's Channel the Eclipse has \"Play the top card of the villain deck\".&nbsp; Atlantean Font of Power -&nbsp; \"At the end of the environment turn, play the top card of the villain deck.&nbsp; Play the top card of each hero deck in turn order.&nbsp; If the played card is a limited card already in play, discard that card and play the next card.&nbsp;&nbsp; At the start of the environment turn, destroy this card.\"&nbsp; So the way it is worded, play the top card is the same as play the next card.&nbsp; And both of those are synonymous with Play a card.&nbsp; For the heroes, it is worded that way in order to distinguish playing a card from your hand.&nbsp;</p><p>So unless&nbsp;someone can point to a clarification somewhere that states that playing the top card is not the same as playing a card off the deck, then I'll have to go with Spiff's clarifications.&nbsp; Thanks in advance.</p>",
      "post_number": 8,
      "post_type": 1,
      "updated_at": "2021-02-07T04:06:24.822Z",
